  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nCurrent conditions/Tonight:\n\nSurface high pressure has set up residency over the Northland \nand will be a mainstay through the weekend and early next week. \nSatellite shows the morning stratus burning off across northern \nMN with ongoing lake effect snow showers continuing across the \nUP and advecting west. Our 850 mb temps still remain around -8C \nwhich leads to lake induced instability of around 200 J/kg. \nCurrent surface dewpoint depressions over the South Shore are \naround 8-10F which is too dry for precipitation. But upstream \nwhere some greenish hues can be depicted in the day cloud phase \nRGB show dewpoint depressions are on the order of 1-3F with \nongoing snow. This activity should cross over into Iron and \nAshland late this afternoon before dry synoptic air and a wind \nshift shuts the process down this evening. Minimal accumulation \nexpected. \n\nWeekend - Mid Week:\n\nQuiet weather period as upper level ridge sets up over the \nUpper Midwest through an extended period. Temperatures will \ngradually be on the upwards trend with highs ascending into the \n50s by Wednesday. For context, normal highs for this time of the\nyear are in the upper 30s. Current NBM guidance even has some \nrecord breaking warm lows for Thursday with values in the low \n40s. \n\nEnd of next work week:\n\nWeather pattern shift does not look to arrive until late \nWednesday into Thursday as troughing in the west finally pushes \nthe upper level ridge out of the region. Forecast trends \ncontinue to slow the advancement of these synoptic features as \nwell as push the associated surface low farther north. As such, \nNBM PoPs continue to be delayed with 30% chances returning late \nWednesday into Thursday. Given the strong dry air in place and \nthe inconsistency of model to model runs confidence remains low \nin the timing and placement of rain returning to the Northland.",
